conclusion += ' Adding unchanged cases produced no clear improvement at fixed 50/50: the paired 95% interval is −0.212 to +0.086 percentage points for embeddings alone, and −0.074 to +0.196 for embeddings plus numerical features. Under the three shared tuned anchors, embeddings alone declined by 0.081 points and the combined model changed by only +0.010 points. The existing numerical selector remains the stronger reference. These data do not support replacing it with an embedding selector on the basis of this experiment.'
parts=['<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>All-validation versus disagreement-only selectors</title><style>body{font:16px system-ui;max-width:1200px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 22px;color:#24343e}h1,h2{color:#24556b}p,li{line-height:1.6}table{border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;margin:18px 0}td,th{padding:8px 11px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;text-align:right}td:first-child,th:first-child{text-align:left}img{width:100%}.note{background:#edf4f7;padding:16px}</style></head><body><h1>Does training on unchanged cases help the NN selector?</h1><p>Aegis, BeaverTails, ToxicChat and ToxiGen · 10 judges · 5 backbones · 200 evaluation cells.</p>',f'<div class="note">{conclusion}</div>','<h2>What changed</h2><p>The previous classifier learned only from validation disagreements. This version learns three outcomes from all paired-valid validation cases: helpful (wrong → correct), harmful (correct → wrong), and unchanged. Correct → correct and wrong → wrong are both unchanged. Cases below the probe referral cutoff are also unchanged because the explanation cannot alter the pipeline decision.</p><p>At inference, the inclusion score is P(helpful) − P(harmful). Logistic regression uses normalized one-vs-rest class probabilities; weighted k-NN uses class proportions. We tune the inclusion cutoff for weighted pipeline F1 using out-of-fold validation predictions. The unchanged class is not mislabeled harmful, and the original sampling weights are retained without class rebalancing.</p>','<h2>Fixed probe/judge thresholds of 50</h2><img src="strategy_comparison.png">',comparison('Fixed 50/50'),'<h2>Paired uncertainty</h2><p>F1 changes are percentage points. Conditional 95% intervals use 2,000 target-cluster multiplier replicates shared across judges/backbones, calibrated within original sampling strata; census strata remain fixed. They exclude training/selection uncertainty and multiple-comparison correction.</p>',table(ci[ci.method.isin(['embedding','combined','selected'])]),'<h2>By judge</h2><img src="judge_deltas.png">']
for group in ['model','dataset','backbone']:
 zz=j[(j.regime=='Fixed 50/50')&j.method.isin(['embedding','combined'])].groupby([group,'method'])[['f1_disagreement','f1_all','delta_f1_pp']].mean().reset_index();zz[['f1_disagreement','f1_all']]*=100;parts+=['<h3>'+group.title()+'</h3>',table(zz)]
parts+=['<h2>Three tuned threshold anchors averaged</h2><p>Each control-, NN- and saliency-selected probe/judge pair remains shared between explanation conditions. Both training strategies use exactly the same frozen pipeline thresholds and test cases.</p>',comparison('Three anchors averaged'),'<h2>Training labels</h2>',table(counts),f'<p>Median eligible validation rows per backbone–judge group: {train[train.policy=="fixed50"].training_rows.median():.0f}; median disagreement rows: {train[train.policy=="fixed50"].training_disagreements.median():.1f}. Counts above are judge–context rows, not independent texts. The same text may appear for multiple judges and backbones.</p>','<h2>Controlled design and limits</h2><p>One selector per backbone/judge pools the four datasets. We hold fixed the previous validation-selected representation for each feature/learner family and policy: embedding logistic, combined logistic, embedding k-NN, combined k-NN, and numerical logistic. The original C or k grid and dataset-specific inclusion cutoff are reselected on validation; PCA projections and grouped folds are unchanged. The previous disagreement-only winners are present in this restricted architecture set. No test outcomes selected any new model or cutoff. Some high-dimensional logistic fits use an equivalent double-precision row-space parameterization for speed; coefficients are mapped back to original coordinates. This does not introduce a new PCA setting or change the model objective. Completed fits from before this implementation optimization are preserved.</p><p>This isolates the effect of adding unchanged examples, including the natural extension from binary to three-class fitting. It is not a new search over all representations optimized specifically for the three-class task. The project has already examined this test set, so these results are exploratory rather than fresh confirmation. Original local population denominators and API retained-support denominators are preserved. No new LLM calls were made.</p><p>Both training classes and all outputs are preserved. The evaluation freezes all new choices before testing and replays prior reference metrics. Per-cell scores, predictions, selected cutoffs and training counts are available below.</p><ul><li><a href="strategy_summary.csv">Overall comparisons</a></li><li><a href="paired_cell_metrics.csv">All paired cell results</a></li><li><a href="paired_intervals.csv">Paired uncertainty</a></li><li><a href="training_counts.csv">Training counts per selector</a></li><li><a href="label_counts.csv">Class counts</a></li><li><a href="raw_outcome_changes.csv">Unweighted corrections and harms</a></li><li><a href="strategy_comparison.pdf">Figure PDF</a></li></ul></body></html>']
